bejoinpass_mips32_O2, bejoinpass_ppc32_O2.o -- MIPS32 and PowerPC big-endian
images for option bejoinpass: a register pair a call hands back and the
function returns untouched (GH-904; tests/stages/kuna-bejoinpass.xml and
tests/cli/bejoinpass-*.json).

What they are
  Both from bejoinpass.c. add64 is proven 64-bit (store writes both words of
  its result). hi is `u32 hi(..) { return add64(..) >> 32; }` and wrap a u64
  wrapper of add64 that reads a volatile after the call, so neither is a tail
  call: both are `jal add64; ..; jr $31` (`bl add64; ..; blr`) with the pair
  left where the call put it, the same bytes. viahook calls through the
  function pointer hook, keeps the pair across ext(), and returns it; usehook
  reads both words of viahook's result, which proves viahook under option
  bejoin callers while hook's callee stays unknown.

Why they are vendored
  CI has no MIPS or PowerPC toolchain, and the assertions depend on the exact
  register choices.

Where they came from
  Ubuntu gcc 10.3.0 (mipsel-linux-gnu-gcc, -EB), Ubuntu binutils 2.38
  (mipsel-linux-gnu-ld, -EB) and Ubuntu clang 14.0.0-1ubuntu1.1 on the host.
  ext() is in its own translation unit, bejoinpass_ext.c, so gcc keeps the
  pair across the call instead of trusting ext's clobbers:

      mipsel-linux-gnu-gcc -EB -fno-pic -mno-abicalls -O2 -w -c bejoinpass.c -o bejoinpass.o
      mipsel-linux-gnu-gcc -EB -fno-pic -mno-abicalls -O2 -w -c bejoinpass_ext.c -o bejoinpass_ext.o
      mipsel-linux-gnu-ld -EB -static --build-id=none -e store \
          bejoinpass.o bejoinpass_ext.o -o bejoinpass_mips32_O2
      clang -target powerpc-linux-gnu -fno-pic -O2 -w -c bejoinpass.c -o bejoinpass_ppc32_O2.o

